 To add IDs to HTML elements in your React component, you can use the `htmlFor` attribute for `label` elements and the `id` attribute for the corresponding input or other HTML elements. Here's how you can modify your code to include IDs:

```jsx
export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model }: { model: CreateObjectModel }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="asset-select">Asset:</label>
      <Select id="asset-select" value={model.selectedAsset} onChange={event => model.setSelectedAsset(event.target.value)}>
        <option value="">-- Select an asset --</option>
        {model.availableAssets.map(asset => (
          <option key={asset.id} value={asset.id}>
            {asset.name}
          </option>
        ))}
      </Select>

      <button onClick={() => {
        if (model.selectedAsset) {
          model.createObject(model.selectedAsset.id).then()
          model.setSelectedAsset(model.selectedAsset)
        }
      }}>
        Add Object
      </button>

      <button onClick={model.cleanupScene}>Cleanup scene</button>
    </div>
  )
})
```

In this example, I added an ID "asset-select" to the label element and used the `key` prop with a unique ID for each option in the dropdown list. This way, the label is associated with the correct select element through the `htmlFor` attribute, and each option has a unique identifier using the `key` prop.

This modification will not affect the performance of your application significantly, but it does make the HTML more accessible and easier to understand for screen readers and developers. Additionally, it will help search engines index the content better. Overall, adding IDs to HTML elements is a good practice for maintaining cleaner and more readable code.

