 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you'll first need to verify the token and then extract the claims containing the username. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Install `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package:
   To work with JWT tokens in C#, install the Microsoft IdentityModel.Tokens NuGet package using the following command:
   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. Create a method to validate JWT:
   Create a new method named `ValidateToken` that will take a JWT token as a parameter and return the claims principal if valid, otherwise throw an exception.

   ```csharp
   using System;
   using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
   using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
   using Microsoft.Extensions.Primitives;

   public static ClaimsPrincipal GetClaimsPrincipalFromExpiredJwt(string jwt)
   {
       try
       {
           var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
           var key = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes("your_secret_key"); // Replace with your secret key

           tokenHandler.ValidateToken(jwt, new TokenValidationParameters
           {
               ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
               IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(key),
               ValidateIssuer = false,
               ValidateAudience = false,
               AllowsExpired = true, // Allow expired tokens for testing purposes
               ValidateLifetime = false // Allow expired tokens for testing purposes
           }, out var claimsPrincipal);

           return claimsPrincipal;
       }
       catch (Exception e)
       {
           throw new ArgumentException("Invalid or expired JWT token", nameof(jwt));
       }
   }
   ```

3. Create a method to get the username:
