 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebAPI, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package for handling JWT tokens in your project. You can install it via the Package Manager Console by running this command:

   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. In your API controller or middleware, create a method to validate and extract the username from the incoming JWT token. Here's an example of how to achieve that:

   ```csharp
   using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
   using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
   using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
   using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
   using System.Security.Claims;
   using System.Text;

   [Route("api/[controller]")]
   [ApiController]
   public class YourController : ControllerBase
   {
       // ... Other methods ...

       [Authorize] // Ensure the request has a valid JWT token
       [HttpGet]
       public ActionResult<string> GetUsername()
       {
           var identity = HttpContext.User.Identity as ClaimsIdentity;

           if (identity != null && identity.IsAuthenticated)
           {
               var jwtToken = ((JwtSecurityToken)identity.AuthenticationType).RawData;
               var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
               var jwtTokenDeserializationOptions = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler().DefaultIncomingSecurityTokenDeserializationParameters { ValidateIssuerSigningKey = false };
               var claimsPrincipal = handler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken);

               return Ok(claimsPrincipal.FindFirstValue(x => x.Type == "sub") ?? "");
           }

           return Unauthorized();
       }
   }
   ```

   This example uses the `Authorize` attribute to secure the endpoint with JWT authentication. The `GetUsername` method retrieves the username from the JWT token by deserializing it and looking up the `sub` claim which typically contains the username.

3. You can now call this endpoint to get the username: `GET api

 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebAPI project, follow these steps:

First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package. If not, install it using the following command in your terminal or Package Manager Console:

```bash
Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
```

Next, create a helper method to validate and extract the username from the JWT token:

1. Create a new class called `JwtSecurityTokenHandlerExtensions.cs` in the root of your project or within the App_Start folder.
2. Add the following code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public static class JwtSecurityTokenHandlerExtensions
{
    public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(this JwtSecurityTokenHandler handler, string jwtToken)
    {
        try
        {
            var jwtTokenValidated = handler.ValidateToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
            {
                ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
                IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("YourSecretKey")),
                ValidateIssuer = false,
                ValidateAudience = false
            }, out _);

            return jwtTokenValidated.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            throw new ApplicationException("Invalid JWT token", ex);
        }
    }
}
```

Replace "YourSecretKey" with the actual secret key used for signing the JWT tokens.

Now you can use the helper method to extract the username from a JWT token in your controller action:

