 To auto-prefix a string before using it as a regular expression in JavaScript, you can simply concatenate the prefix to the string before creating the new RegExp object. Here's an example:

```javascript
let prefix = 'auto-'; // Define your prefix here
let inputString = '/regex/'; // Your original string
let pattern = new RegExp('^' + prefix + inputString); // Create the new pattern with the prefix

// Usage
if (pattern.test('someString')) {
  console.log('Match found');
} else {
  console.log('No match');
}
```

In this example, we define a `prefix` variable with the value 'auto-'. We also have an `inputString` variable containing the original regex pattern ('/regex/' in this case). To create the new pattern with the prefix, we use the `^` character at the beginning of the pattern to ensure the matching starts from the beginning of the string. We then concatenate the prefix and the original pattern using the `+` operator. Finally, we create a new `RegExp` object with the newly formed pattern.

