 To auto-prefix a string before using it as a regular expression in JavaScript, you can concatenate the prefix string with your regex pattern before creating the new RegExp object. Here's an example:

```javascript
const prefix = "autoPrefix"; // Define your prefix here
const regexPattern = /someRegexPattern/; // Define your regex pattern here

// Concatenate prefix and regex pattern
const regexWithPrefix = new RegExp(`${prefix}${regexPattern.source}`);

// Now use the new regex with prefix
const match = regexWithPrefix.exec("inputString");
if (match) {
  console.log(match[0]);
}
```

In this example, replace `"autoPrefix"` with your desired prefix string and `"someRegexPattern"` with your actual regex pattern. The `${prefix}${regexPattern.source}` syntax is used to concatenate the prefix and regex pattern strings, then create a new RegExp object from that combined string.

This approach ensures that the prefix is added to your regex pattern before executing the search.

