 It looks like the issue is that your `generateRandomPlanets` function is generating planets too close to each other. To prevent this, you can modify the function to keep generating new planets until they are all within a certain minimum distance from each other. Here's an updated version of the function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    let tooClose = false;
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const otherPlanet = planets[i];
      for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        const distance = Math.abs(planet.position[j] - otherPlanet.position[j]);
        if (distance < minDistance) {
          tooClose = true;
          break;
        }
      }
      if (tooClose) {
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
}
```

This updated function checks the distance between the newly generated planet and all existing planets before adding it to the list. If the new planet is too close to any existing one, it continues generating new planets until it finds one that is far enough away. The loop that checks distances is nested inside another loop, so it only checks distances for nearby dimensions, which should help improve performance.

Additionally, I changed the name of the variable `tooClose` to be more descriptive and moved its initialization outside of the inner loop to avoid unnecessary re-initializations.

