 I. Introduction

In this PowerShell script, we will recursively check the Access Control Lists (ACLs) of folders in an Active Directory domain to identify any Access Control Entries (ACEs) that reference Security Identifiers (SIDs) which only exist in the SID History of any user or group in the domain.

II. Prerequisites

Before running the script, ensure you have the following:

1. PowerShell installed on your machine.
2. The Active Directory module for PowerShell (RSAT-ADDS-PowerShell) installed.
3. Run PowerShell with Administrative privileges.
4. Set Execution Policy to "RemoteSigned" or "Unrestricted" to run scripts downloaded from the internet.

III. Script

```powershell
# Set variables
$DomainName        = "yourdomain.com"
$RootPath          = "C:\Path\To\Root\Folder"
$OutputCSVFilePath = "C:\Path\To\Output.csv"

# Function to retrieve SID History for a given User or Group
function Get-SidHistory {
    param([string]$SamAccountName)

    $UserOrGroup = Get-ADObject -Filter "SamAccountName -eq '$SamAccountName'" -Properties SID,SIDHistory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    if (!$UserOrGroup) {
        Write-Host ("User or Group '{0}' not found." -f $SamAccountName) -ForegroundColor Yellow
        return @()
    }

    $Sid = $UserOrGroup.SID
    $SidHistory = $UserOrGroup.SIDHistory
    return ($Sid, $SidHistory)
}

# Function to check if an SID is in the output array
function Check-SidInArray {
    param([array]$Array, [string]$Sid)

    $Found = $false
    foreach ($item in $Array) {
        if ($item[0] -eq $Sid) {
            $Found = $true
            break
        }
    }

    return $Found
}

